.TH JOIN 1 2026-10-27 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
join \- join two sorted files on a common field
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
.RB [ \-a
.I fnum
.RB "| " \-v
.IR fnum ]...\&
.RB [ \-e
.IR str ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR sep ]
.RB [ \-1
.IR field ]
.RB [ \-2
.IR field ]
.I file1
.I file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B join
combines the lines of two files that have a field in common.
It writes one line for each pair of a line of
.I file1
and a line of
.I file2
whose join fields are equal.
Both files must be sorted on the join field.
Either file may be
.BR \- ,
for standard input, but not both.
.PP
The join field is field 1 of each line, unless
.B \-1
or
.B \-2
chooses another field of
.I file1
or
.IR file2 .
.SS Fields
Without
.BR \-t ,
blanks, meaning spaces and tabs, separate fields.
A run of blanks is one separator, and blanks at the start or end of a
line are ignored, so no field is empty and a blank line has no fields.
With
.BR \-t ,
each occurrence of the separator character ends a field.
Fields can then be empty, and a line has one more field than it has
separators.
A line with fewer fields than the join field number has an empty join
field, which matches the empty join field of a line in the other file.
.SS Joining
.B join
reads the files in order, and compares the join fields of their current
lines as byte strings, not by the collation order of a locale.
Consecutive lines of a file with equal join fields form a group.
.PP
When the join fields of two groups are equal,
.B join
writes the output described below for every pairing of a line of the
group of
.I file1
with a line of the group of
.IR file2 :
for each line of
.IR file1 ,
all the lines of
.IR file2 ,
in order.
Groups of 2 and 3 lines give 6 output lines.
It then goes on to the next group of both files.
.PP
Otherwise it goes on to the next group of the file with the lesser join
field, first writing the lines of the group it leaves as unpaired lines
if
.B \-a
or
.B \-v
asks for the unpaired lines of that file.
When one file ends, the groups left in the other are handled in the same
way.
.PP
The order of the input is not checked.
Where the files are not sorted, lines that should pair are skipped or
written as unpaired lines, with no message and exit status 0.
.SS Output
Every output line ends with LF.
Without
.BR \-o ,
a joined line is the join field, then the fields of the
.I file1
line other than its join field, then those of the
.I file2
line, separated by a space or, with
.BR \-t ,
by the separator character.
An unpaired line is written the same way from its own file alone, so its
join field moves to the front.
With
.BR \-o ,
the fields named by the list are written instead.
An empty field is written as the
.B \-e
string, which is empty by default.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option takes an argument, which may be attached, as in
.BR \-a1 ,
or be the next argument, so options cannot be grouped.
Options may follow the operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A repeated option replaces its earlier values, except
.B \-a
and
.BR \-v ,
which accumulate.
.TP
.BI "\-a " fnum
Also write the unpaired lines of file number
.IR fnum ,
which is
.B 1
or
.BR 2 ,
among the joined lines in input order.
Repeat the option to write the unpaired lines of both files.
Incompatible with
.BR \-v .
.TP
.BI "\-v " fnum
Like
.BR \-a ,
but write only unpaired lines: the joined lines are not written.
Incompatible with
.BR \-a .
.TP
.BI "\-e " str
Write
.I str
in place of each empty field.
This includes an empty join field, and with
.B \-o
each listed field that the line does not have.
Without
.BR \-o ,
fields that a line does not have are not written at all.
The default is the empty string.
.TP
.BI "\-o " list
Write the fields named by
.I list
instead of the default output, in the order given, separated like the
default output.
The items of
.I list
are separated by commas, spaces, or tabs.
An item is
.B 0
for the join field, or
.BI 1. n
or
.BI 2. n
for field
.I n
of the
.I file1
or
.I file2
line, where
.I n
is a decimal number from 1 to 2147483647.
An item may be repeated.
A field the line does not have is written as the
.B \-e
string, as is every field of the other file in an unpaired line.
On an unpaired line of
.IR file2 ,
.B 0
is the join field of
.IR file2 .
A list with no items is accepted, and makes every output line empty.
.TP
.BI "\-t " sep
Separate fields with the character
.I sep
in the input and the output, instead of runs of blanks.
.I sep
must be at most one byte, so a non-ASCII character is an error where
arguments are UTF-8, as on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
An empty
.I sep
is the same as no
.BR \-t .
It is taken literally, with no backslash escapes.
Pass a tab as a tab character.
.TP
.BI "\-1 " field
Use field number
.I field
of
.I file1
as the join field.
It is a decimal number from 1 to 2147483647.
The default is 1.
.TP
.BI "\-2 " field
Use field number
.I field
of
.I file2
as the join field.
The range and default are those of
.BR \-1 .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments, including option arguments, are expanded
against file names at startup, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, whether or not any line was written.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line, such as an unknown option, an invalid option
argument, both
.B \-a
and
.BR \-v ,
or other than two operands; a file could not be opened, or is a
directory; writing the output still buffered at exit failed; or out of
memory.
A message or the usage is written to standard error.
.TP
.B 141
A write to standard output failed because it is a pipe whose reader has
exited.
When this happens only at exit, while writing the output still buffered,
the status is 1 instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Join two files on their first field, writing only the keys found in
both:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat names.txt
1 alice
2 bob
3 carol
$ cat ages.txt
1 34
3 27
4 51
$ join names.txt ages.txt
1 alice 34
3 carol 27
.EE
.RE
.PP
Also write the lines of the first file that have no match, with a
placeholder for the missing age:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ join \-a 1 \-e unknown \-o 0,1.2,2.2 names.txt ages.txt
1 alice 34
2 bob unknown
3 carol 27
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write only the lines of the first file that have no match:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ join \-v 1 names.txt ages.txt
2 bob
.EE
.RE
.PP
Join comma-separated files on the second field of the first and the
first field of the second, sorting the first file on its way in:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat orders.csv
o1,2,pen
o2,1,ink
o3,2,pad
$ cat customers.csv
1,alice
2,bob
$ sort \-t, \-k2,2 orders.csv | join \-t, \-1 2 \-2 1 \- customers.csv
1,o2,ink,alice
2,o1,pen,bob
2,o3,pad,bob
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU join:
.IP \(bu 2
The only long option is
.BR \-\-help :
there is no
.BR \-\-check\-order ,
.BR \-\-nocheck\-order ,
or
.BR \-\-header ,
and no
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-j ,
or
.BR \-z .
.B \-o
has no
.B auto
form.
.IP \(bu 2
The order of the input is never checked.
.IP \(bu 2
Join fields are compared as bytes, not in the collation order of the
locale.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-a
and
.B \-v
cannot be combined.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
takes at most one byte.
An empty argument, which makes each line one field in GNU join, here
means that blanks separate fields, as without
.BR \-t .
.PP
Without
.BR \-t ,
when the last line of a file has no newline and does not end in a blank,
.B join
reads past the end of that line in memory, and the line can gain extra
fields of garbage bytes, which are written and can even be its join
field.
End the last line with a newline.
.PP
Only memory limits the length of a line and the number of fields.
.PP
Files are read as binary.
A CR directly before an LF is removed from each input line, so files with
CR LF and LF line endings behave alike, and output lines end with LF.
Any other CR, such as one ending an unterminated last line, is an
ordinary character.
A NUL byte ends a line, as LF does, and is not written.
The last line of a file need not end in a newline, except as described
above.
A read error ends a file silently.
.PP
.I file1
and
.I file2
may be Windows paths, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or a
busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names and arguments are UTF-8
and may contain any Unicode characters, and on older systems only those
of the system code page.
.B join
handles text as bytes.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR comm (1),
.BR paste (1),
.BR sort (1)
